\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

\code{grimmer_values()} returns every pair of a mean and an SD that a sample of
\code{n} integers can have and that would be reported as \code{x} and \code{sd}. It
is the counterpart of \code{\link[=grim_values]{grim_values()}} for \code{\link[=grimmer]{grimmer()}}, which is \code{TRUE}
exactly if there is at least one such pair.

\code{grimmer_closest()} returns the single achievable pair whose SD is
closest to \code{sd}, like \code{\link[=grim_closest]{grim_closest()}} for means. If \code{x} and \code{sd} are
consistent, it is one of the pairs that \code{grimmer_values()} returns.
Otherwise, it is closest among the pairs whose mean would be reported as
\code{x}, or, if there are none, whose mean is the one \code{grim_closest()}
returns.

Each pair comes with the whole-number sum and sum of squares behind it.
Statistics computed from several groups, such as a t-value, can be
compared exactly through these integers.
}
\details{
The sums are the whole numbers that \code{\link[=grim_values]{grim_values()}} divides by
\code{n * items_averaged}. For each, the sums of squares are the whole numbers
within the rounding bounds of \code{sd} that \code{n} integers with that sum can
have. With \code{items_averaged > 1}, the integers are the respondents'
totals across the items, so the sums are those of the totals.

The number of pairs grows with \code{n}, \code{items_averaged}, and \code{sd}, and
shrinks with \code{digits_x} and \code{digits_sd}. More than a million pairs for one
value set is an error, since the output has one row for each. So is a sum
of squares of 2^53 or more, about 9e15, which a double can't hold exactly;
\code{\link[=grimmer]{grimmer()}} tests such value sets all the same. \code{grimmer_closest()}
stops at 2^48, about 3e14, beyond which it can miss the closest pair.
With \code{n} from 2 to 4, both stop at 2^40, about 1.1e12, as \code{\link[=grimmer]{grimmer()}}
does, \code{grimmer_closest()} even if \code{x} fails GRIM.

Under \code{rounding = "even"}, the pairs include ties that \code{\link[base:Round]{base::round()}}
may round away from \code{x} or \code{sd}, as in \code{\link[=grim_values]{grim_values()}}.

In \code{grimmer_closest()}, pairs whose SDs are equally close go by the mean
closer to \code{x}, then by the smaller sum, then by the smaller SD. A negative
\code{sd} is closest to the least SD there is. The number of pairs doesn't
matter there, but that of the sums does: for a consistent value set, it
tries one sum per remainder modulo \code{n}, and more than 100,000 of them is
an error. That takes an \code{n} of more than about \code{10^(digits_x + 5)}.
